-- erwartet: H102
-- **The same deadlock as `gift/460`, one word further away -- and until
-- 2026-09-28 that one word bought silence.**
--
-- `gift/460` is refused because its entry says `via idt` and takes a lock that
-- does not declare `masks irqs`. This file is that file with `idt` replaced by
-- `irq`, and nothing else: the hardware still throws the entry, the interrupted
-- path may still be holding `TAKT`, and the handler would still wait for a
-- holder that only resumes once the handler returns. **Measured before the
-- widening: 0 errors.** `Kontext::unterbricht` compared the path against the
-- literal `"idt"`, so any other spelling -- a real second path, or a typo --
-- turned `H102` off without a word.
--
-- *A measuring instrument that goes quiet on a typo is the `W16` class*, and
-- this file is the witness that it does not any more: the trigger is now that a
-- `via` word stands there at all.
--
-- WHY `irq` AND NOT A MISSPELLING. Both would fall now, but only one of them is
-- a shape somebody writes on purpose. A Gabbro unit built as a Linux kernel
-- module is entered from the HOST kernel's interrupt path: a timer, a device
-- line, a softirq -- none of which owns a vector the program could name, so
-- `via idt vector N` is the wrong word for it and `via irq` is the right one
-- (`messung/proben/kmodul/sperre-takt.gab` is the running probe). The refusal
-- has to reach that spelling, or the module target has no guard at all.
--
-- The remedy is the counter-probe `beispiele/166-eintritt-irq-maskiert.gab`:
-- the same file with `masks irqs`, clean -- so this is a rule and not a ban on
-- the word.

module gift {

table Takte count 64 {
    slot { stand : u64, }
}

-- **The lock without `masks irqs`** -- the whole difference to the example.
lock TAKT protects { Takte } rank 0 held <= 40 ops;

impl fn zaehle(i : index into Takte)
    requires Held(TAKT)
    effects  { reads Takte, reads Takte.slots, writes Takte.slots, locks TAKT }
    costs    <= 8 ops
{
    Takte.slots[i].stand = 1;
}

impl fn takt_verteiler()
    effects { reads Takte, reads Takte.slots, writes Takte.slots, locks TAKT }
    costs   <= 40 ops
{
    locks TAKT {
        zaehle(0);
    }
}

-- **`via irq` -- the host kernel's interrupt path, not the x86 IDT.** No
-- vector: the program does not choose which line the kernel hands it, and a
-- number here would be a claim it cannot keep. `nested never` covers the vector
-- re-entering itself, not the holder that was just interrupted.
entry zeitgeber via irq arch x86_64 {
    regs in  { }
    regs out { }
    preserves { rbx, rbp, r12, r13, r14, r15 }
    clobbers  { rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8, r9, r10, r11 }
    stack takt_stapel per cpu nested never
    dispatch gift::takt_verteiler;
}

}
